1
00:00:53,410 --> 00:00:56,010
For now, we'll have to work together
toward the same goal, right?

2
00:00:58,310 --> 00:01:02,120
Let me perform a medical demonstration
using my past patients' records.

3
00:01:05,320 --> 00:01:09,230
It'll be hard to get them to agree
with just those records.

4
00:01:09,730 --> 00:01:11,360
In fact, if those records go public,

5
00:01:11,690 --> 00:01:15,500
they'll demand that you produce
the cure you used.

6
00:01:16,300 --> 00:01:17,700
That's what I'm counting on.

7
00:01:19,340 --> 00:01:20,340
What?

8
00:01:22,310 --> 00:01:23,310
Don't tell me...

9
00:01:25,480 --> 00:01:27,280
You've had the cure all along?

10
00:01:29,810 --> 00:01:30,810
Yes.

11
00:01:35,550 --> 00:01:39,890
(The Flower of Judgment)

12
00:01:40,190 --> 00:01:42,790
(Current Affairs Takedown)

13
00:01:43,160 --> 00:01:46,100
The witnesses gave
conflicting testimony, didn't they?

14
00:01:46,700 --> 00:01:47,760
Of course.

15
00:01:48,260 --> 00:01:52,040
Seriously, who on earth would undergo
some unverified treatment...

16
00:01:52,040 --> 00:01:53,400
and be completely cured?

17
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:58,840
When you see that, you have to wonder
if there's a flaw in the legal system...

18
00:01:59,040 --> 00:02:00,510
that indulges the defendant's demands.

19
00:02:00,580 --> 00:02:02,210
(Current Affairs Forum)

20
00:02:03,550 --> 00:02:05,180
What a mess. What a total mess.

21
00:02:07,020 --> 00:02:09,790
Nothing about this trial
is going as planned.

22
00:02:11,250 --> 00:02:15,930
I'm going to get dragged through the mud
because of Prosecutor Cha. Darn it.

23
00:02:16,430 --> 00:02:19,160
Hey, Min Gyeong,
you know Prosecutor Cha, right?

24
00:02:19,530 --> 00:02:22,700
This is my first time seeing her in court.
I've seen her on social media, though.

25
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:25,170
She's got a great resume,
plus she's gorgeous.

26
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:26,570
And her family's rich on top of that.

27
00:02:26,870 --> 00:02:27,970
Maybe that's why...

28
00:02:29,140 --> 00:02:30,270
she's so audacious.

29
00:02:31,270 --> 00:02:33,980
It's not audacity. She's just plain rude.

30
00:02:35,110 --> 00:02:37,910
But still, I have to admit
she's pretty impressive.

31
00:02:38,180 --> 00:02:39,780
Impressive, my foot.

32
00:02:41,450 --> 00:02:42,450
Whatever...

33
00:03:07,180 --> 00:03:10,580
You see your dad for the first time
in ages,

34
00:03:10,810 --> 00:03:13,820
and you don't even say hello,
much less act happy to see me?

35
00:03:16,120 --> 00:03:17,390
I already told you.

36
00:03:18,050 --> 00:03:20,320
I told you not to come here,
no matter what.

37
00:03:21,990 --> 00:03:23,430
If people see me with you,

38
00:03:23,930 --> 00:03:26,330
they'll start rumors out of thin air,
and I really...

39
00:03:28,430 --> 00:03:30,170
I'm telling you.
I won't be able to do my job.

40
00:03:31,770 --> 00:03:33,900
Good grief, you impossible child.

41
00:03:37,110 --> 00:03:38,670
You make life so difficult for yourself.

42
00:03:39,740 --> 00:03:41,280
Why do you care so much about
what other people think?

43
00:03:41,540 --> 00:03:44,250
Can't a father even see
his own daughter's face?

44
00:03:44,710 --> 00:03:45,720
Can't I?

45
00:03:46,380 --> 00:03:48,450
The problem is that
you're not just any dad.

46
00:03:50,590 --> 00:03:53,520
I have to get back inside.
Please, just tell me what you came for.

47
00:03:54,090 --> 00:03:56,060
Anything but telling me
to join the company.

48
00:03:56,990 --> 00:04:00,030
What if that's the only thing
I came to say?

49
00:04:01,730 --> 00:04:06,200
In your view, having your daughter
help people in difficult situations...

50
00:04:06,540 --> 00:04:08,740
is better for the company's image,
isn't it?

51
00:04:09,170 --> 00:04:11,570
When did I ever force you to live for me?

52
00:04:11,770 --> 00:04:14,440
Then why do you keep asking me
to join the company?

53
00:04:14,810 --> 00:04:16,950
Because I worry about you! You.

54
00:04:17,610 --> 00:04:18,610
After all this time?

55
00:04:21,750 --> 00:04:22,920
Fine. You don't have to join the company.

56
00:04:24,250 --> 00:04:25,290
On one condition.

57
00:04:26,090 --> 00:04:27,190
Drop this case.

58
00:04:29,890 --> 00:04:32,630
(Jaeil Hospital)

59
00:04:33,960 --> 00:04:34,960
Yes?

60
00:04:37,170 --> 00:04:38,170
Hey.

61
00:04:40,500 --> 00:04:42,000
What brings you here all of a sudden?
You didn't even call.

62
00:04:42,370 --> 00:04:44,140
It's just that...

63
00:04:45,310 --> 00:04:48,080
These are the medical records
of the patients the defendant treated,

64
00:04:48,340 --> 00:04:49,380
and I was hoping...

65
00:04:50,580 --> 00:04:51,680
you could take a look.

66
00:04:55,180 --> 00:04:57,920
What were you thinking,
defending a man like that?

67
00:04:59,960 --> 00:05:01,660
It's partly for Min Seo. And also...

68
00:05:02,160 --> 00:05:05,190
I need the legal fees to pay for
Min Seo's hospital bills.

69
00:05:05,260 --> 00:05:09,470
- I told you not to trust that man.
- I know, okay? I know.

70
00:05:09,970 --> 00:05:14,370
But if it's true that Lee Woo Gyeom
cured patients with incurable diseases...

71
00:05:15,300 --> 00:05:17,740
- Then maybe our Min Seo...
- Please, just stop!

72
00:05:17,740 --> 00:05:19,210
It hasn't even been that long
since you were last scammed!

73
00:05:19,380 --> 00:05:21,580
You'd entrust our Min Seo
to a murderer like him?

74
00:05:21,910 --> 00:05:23,310
I'm a doctor!

75
00:05:23,610 --> 00:05:26,780
Stop making decisions about Min Seo
without me! Please!

76
00:05:57,650 --> 00:06:00,380
(Current Affairs Forum)

77
00:06:00,550 --> 00:06:03,550
(Prosecutor Cha Yi Yeon)

78
00:06:07,560 --> 00:06:08,860
On this episode
of the Current Affairs Forum...

79
00:06:08,920 --> 00:06:13,500
He prosecuted and arrested Lee Seong Gon,
South Korea's last death row inmate.

80
00:06:13,900 --> 00:06:18,370
We're joined by Assemblyman Baek Sang Hwa
for his thoughts on capital punishment.

81
00:06:19,500 --> 00:06:21,900
- Assemblyman Baek, welcome.
- Thank you for having me.

82
00:06:24,070 --> 00:06:25,140
Assemblyman Baek?

83
00:06:25,510 --> 00:06:27,710
What do you think?
It's a killer plan, isn't it?

84
00:06:28,540 --> 00:06:32,450
So, Assemblyman Baek is going to make
the death penalty a campaign promise,

85
00:06:32,980 --> 00:06:36,590
and you've made a deal for Lee Woo Gyeom's
death sentence. So that's what this is.

86
00:06:37,290 --> 00:06:41,990
We have to pave his way to victory
so we can get our win in this trial. See?

87
00:06:42,330 --> 00:06:44,390
But if he's going to build a platform
on this,

88
00:06:45,290 --> 00:06:47,400
the trial verdict alone won't be enough,
will it?

89
00:06:48,900 --> 00:06:50,630
What? You have a better idea?

90
00:06:53,270 --> 00:06:55,600
Assemblyman,
you should appear on a TV show.

91
00:06:56,010 --> 00:06:57,040
A TV show?

92
00:06:57,810 --> 00:07:00,440
- What show?
- You know Current Affairs Forum, right?

93
00:07:01,310 --> 00:07:03,580
Because of the Lee Woo Gyeom case
I'm currently on,

94
00:07:03,950 --> 00:07:05,850
they're doing a special
on the death penalty.

95
00:07:06,350 --> 00:07:08,250
Wouldn't you be the perfect person
to go on the show?

96
00:07:08,850 --> 00:07:10,590
Me? Why me?

97
00:07:11,190 --> 00:07:12,220
You haven't forgotten, have you?

98
00:07:12,690 --> 00:07:15,490
The country's last death row inmate,
Lee Seong Gon.

99
00:07:15,790 --> 00:07:17,330
What? Lee Seong Gon?

100
00:07:17,930 --> 00:07:20,730
That jerk.
I put him away when I was a prosecutor.

101
00:07:21,230 --> 00:07:24,470
Exactly. Creating a parallel
from Lee Seong Gon to Lee Woo Gyeom.

102
00:07:24,630 --> 00:07:27,040
The 1st execution in 28 years.
It's a parallel narrative.

103
00:07:27,700 --> 00:07:29,840
Who could be a better expert
on the matter than you?

104
00:07:32,840 --> 00:07:35,780
If you can sway public opinion in favor
of the death penalty on the show,

105
00:07:36,380 --> 00:07:39,620
wouldn't that be a huge boost
for your next election?

106
00:07:46,920 --> 00:07:51,830
So you're saying failing to execute
is, in itself, against the law?

107
00:07:51,890 --> 00:07:53,130
That's right.

108
00:07:53,260 --> 00:07:57,430
The law says anyone sentenced to death
must be executed within six months.

109
00:07:57,700 --> 00:07:59,270
It's explicitly written in the statute.

110
00:07:59,940 --> 00:08:02,370
If these executions aren't carried out
in a timely manner,

111
00:08:02,670 --> 00:08:04,940
then the Ministry of Justice
is breaking the law.

112
00:08:05,310 --> 00:08:06,540
That is a dereliction of duty.

113
00:08:07,210 --> 00:08:09,750
But if we look at the past,
there've been politically driven cases...

114
00:08:09,750 --> 00:08:11,980
- Excuse me, you all need to see this.
- where innocent people...

115
00:08:11,980 --> 00:08:13,020
were put on death row?

116
00:08:13,480 --> 00:08:16,450
For a murderer like Lee Woo Gyeom,

117
00:08:17,120 --> 00:08:20,890
if we don't punish him as he deserves,
who's going to fear the law,

118
00:08:20,960 --> 00:08:23,560
and who will bring justice
to these victims?

119
00:08:24,360 --> 00:08:28,360
I understand your sentiment,
Assemblyman Baek, but...

120
00:08:28,430 --> 00:08:31,130
doesn't that risk turning it into
a retributive punishment?

121
00:08:31,500 --> 00:08:34,800
Capital punishment isn't retribution,
it's justice.

122
00:08:35,240 --> 00:08:36,640
The ultimate implementation of justice.

123
00:08:37,710 --> 00:08:41,440
- The ultimate form of justice, you say?
- That's right.

124
00:08:41,710 --> 00:08:43,110
But the even bigger problem is...

125
00:08:43,750 --> 00:08:47,950
people defend the rights of criminals
who ought to be made an example of,

126
00:08:48,380 --> 00:08:52,690
and that some people actually defend
and advocate for these heinous criminals.

127
00:08:52,960 --> 00:08:54,220
That is the problem.

128
00:08:54,460 --> 00:08:58,930
It sounds like you're taking a direct shot
at Lee Woo Gyeom's defense attorney.

129
00:08:59,930 --> 00:09:01,500
Seventeen people were murdered.

130
00:09:02,260 --> 00:09:07,170
Do you know how much he mocked the police
and prosecution during the investigation?

131
00:09:07,740 --> 00:09:09,870
Advocating for a criminal like that...

132
00:09:09,940 --> 00:09:14,180
is not something a sane person does.
It's crazy, that's what it is.

133
00:09:14,240 --> 00:09:15,540
Assemblyman. Sir?

134
00:09:17,810 --> 00:09:21,450
And another thing,
I heard his lawyer recently got a divorce.

135
00:09:22,080 --> 00:09:26,860
You know the saying, "Fix your own house
before you try to fix the world," right?

136
00:09:27,690 --> 00:09:30,430
He suddenly ups and quits
his perfectly good job at a law firm...

137
00:09:30,490 --> 00:09:32,600
and runs around defending
vicious criminals like that,

138
00:09:32,860 --> 00:09:35,230
so is it any wonder
his family life is a mess?

139
00:09:35,300 --> 00:09:38,530
There he goes again.
I can't believe this guy. Goodness.

140
00:09:39,270 --> 00:09:40,400
Counsel for the defense,

141
00:09:40,900 --> 00:09:43,910
if you're watching this, you listen to me
and you listen good. Got it?

142
00:09:44,170 --> 00:09:47,510
Cut the crap about Lee Woo Gyeom
supposedly saving people...

143
00:09:47,580 --> 00:09:48,640
and resign from the case immediately!

144
00:09:49,950 --> 00:09:52,080
(ON-SCREEN TEXT: Prosecutor Cha Yi Yeon)

145
00:10:02,160 --> 00:10:05,630
On last night's Current Affairs Forum,
Assemblyman Baek Sang Hwa...

146
00:10:05,690 --> 00:10:07,700
- Resign, defense attorney!
- strongly supported the death penalty,

147
00:10:07,700 --> 00:10:09,870
- arguing it's an absolute necessity.
- Resign, defense attorney!

148
00:10:10,030 --> 00:10:13,300
He then turned to Park Han Jun,
Lee Woo Gyeom's defense attorney,

149
00:10:13,370 --> 00:10:16,910
and the clip of him shouting "Resign"
is now going viral on social media.

150
00:10:20,380 --> 00:10:21,380
("He's divorced." Personal attacks)

151
00:10:21,440 --> 00:10:22,880
(Trending Number 2,
"Is he really a public official?")

152
00:10:24,710 --> 00:10:26,720
(Lawyers like him let criminals walk.
These people would sell their souls.)

153
00:10:26,920 --> 00:10:28,150
(This country's a mess.
Time to get out of Korea?)

154
00:10:28,220 --> 00:10:29,450
(Expose how much money
Park Han Jun pocketed.)

155
00:10:34,520 --> 00:10:35,560
Mr. Park.

156
00:10:38,430 --> 00:10:42,200
Don't worry about it.
This won't affect the trial at all.

157
00:10:42,800 --> 00:10:46,370
Exactly. These are just cowards
who wouldn't dare say a word to your face.

158
00:10:46,570 --> 00:10:49,170
They just complain safely from home.
Right?

159
00:10:49,970 --> 00:10:51,870
I saw a couple of positive comments,
I guess.

160
00:10:55,740 --> 00:10:57,310
- You did great.
- Thank you, sir.

161
00:11:02,120 --> 00:11:06,320
Prosecutor Cha. Come in, come on in.
Have a seat.

162
00:11:12,800 --> 00:11:13,830
Goodness.

163
00:11:14,900 --> 00:11:19,870
Alright, you two, introduce yourselves.
Let's grab a bite together. Okay?

164
00:11:21,140 --> 00:11:22,170
Prosecutor Cha.

165
00:11:22,740 --> 00:11:24,810
For that TV interview,

166
00:11:25,110 --> 00:11:27,710
Chief Pyo here
gave me a lot of great pointers.

167
00:11:29,450 --> 00:11:30,550
I'm Pyo Kang Won.

168
00:11:30,980 --> 00:11:34,520
Prosecutor,
can I assume we're on the same team now?

169
00:11:35,150 --> 00:11:38,120
- I'm Cha Yi Yeon. Pleasure to meet you.
- Good.

170
00:11:39,660 --> 00:11:41,420
What's this now?

171
00:11:44,990 --> 00:11:48,030
Gosh, this guy always calls
right when I'm trying to eat.

172
00:11:48,360 --> 00:11:49,570
Excuse me for a moment. Okay?

173
00:11:49,970 --> 00:11:51,000
- Go ahead and eat.
- Yes, sir.

174
00:11:51,070 --> 00:11:52,070
- Yes, sir.
- Hello?

175
00:11:52,300 --> 00:11:56,540
Minister Hong. Hey.
I'm in the middle of a meal right now.

176
00:12:00,310 --> 00:12:05,280
Were you the one who engineered
the assemblyman's persona for that show?

177
00:12:07,220 --> 00:12:09,320
Yes, Prosecutor.
You found it entertaining, right?

178
00:12:11,590 --> 00:12:12,760
You thought that was entertaining?

179
00:12:13,620 --> 00:12:15,720
I couldn't even watch it
because it was so pathetic.

180
00:12:16,890 --> 00:12:20,630
- Excuse me?
- If you thought that was a joke,

181
00:12:21,460 --> 00:12:24,170
I should have recommended you
to a comedy show instead.

182
00:12:25,430 --> 00:12:29,510
Look, Prosecutor,
maybe you're too young to understand,

183
00:12:29,910 --> 00:12:31,910
but everything we do on television
is strategic.

184
00:12:32,910 --> 00:12:34,410
If we had only talked about
the death penalty,

185
00:12:34,540 --> 00:12:37,910
it would've been too boring
to grab young people's attention.

186
00:12:38,710 --> 00:12:41,880
And that "strategy" was
to trash-talk the defense attorney?

187
00:12:42,690 --> 00:12:46,690
Thanks to that, the assemblyman's clip
got over five million views.

188
00:12:46,760 --> 00:12:49,220
If you just wanted to rack up views,
you should've worked with a YouTuber.

189
00:12:49,860 --> 00:12:53,260
Why are you pulling these useless stunts
on national television?

190
00:12:54,800 --> 00:12:59,470
Do you realize the top search term now
is Park Han Jun, not the death penalty?

191
00:13:00,100 --> 00:13:04,240
Park Han Jun is getting all the attention
that capital punishment should be getting!

192
00:13:06,110 --> 00:13:07,140
Come on, Prosecutor.

193
00:13:07,780 --> 00:13:10,950
So what, did you expect some grand speech
on reviving the death penalty for justice?

194
00:13:11,410 --> 00:13:13,280
Did you really expect
such a childish remark?

195
00:13:19,320 --> 00:13:20,590
Unbelievable.

196
00:13:26,160 --> 00:13:27,160
Prosecutor.

197
00:13:28,200 --> 00:13:31,830
You're being sensitive about small things
because you're a woman, but...

198
00:13:32,130 --> 00:13:34,740
We know what we're doing in Yeouido.

199
00:13:35,300 --> 00:13:37,110
- Excuse me?
- Look.

200
00:13:40,840 --> 00:13:42,640
The food served is all the same,

201
00:13:43,080 --> 00:13:44,950
so why do you think
we each have our own small plate?

202
00:13:45,650 --> 00:13:49,620
It means you take what you want
and stick to your own plate.

203
00:13:54,820 --> 00:13:57,090
Whether I spoon-feed them
or shove it down their throats...

204
00:13:57,660 --> 00:14:01,400
is up to me, the chef.
What's all this talk about Yeouido?

205
00:14:02,730 --> 00:14:06,770
This all started with Lee Woo Gyeom,
and I'm the prosecutor on his case!

206
00:14:07,940 --> 00:14:11,010
And what does being a man or a woman
have to do with any of this?

207
00:14:11,710 --> 00:14:12,940
Is he nuts? Goodness.

208
00:14:17,080 --> 00:14:18,880
My gosh.

209
00:14:22,350 --> 00:14:24,020
What's going on? What's all this?

210
00:14:24,590 --> 00:14:26,820
Things look pretty heated over here.

211
00:14:28,390 --> 00:14:30,030
- Assemblyman.
- My.

212
00:14:30,630 --> 00:14:34,660
Thanks to your chief of staff,
I've had a real wake-up call.

213
00:14:34,830 --> 00:14:38,800
See?
I had a feeling you two would hit it off.

214
00:14:39,740 --> 00:14:41,900
Alright, let's have a drink! Come on!

215
00:14:43,740 --> 00:14:45,440
This is a great atmosphere tonight.

216
00:14:46,610 --> 00:14:49,010
All right, here we go. Cheers!

217
00:15:00,260 --> 00:15:02,460
Counselor, you need to see this.

218
00:15:02,890 --> 00:15:04,130
What? Did you find something?

219
00:15:04,190 --> 00:15:06,930
You were wondering about the delay
in identifying the victims, right?

220
00:15:07,360 --> 00:15:08,500
Take a look at this.

221
00:15:11,030 --> 00:15:13,640
We did some digging and...

222
00:15:14,640 --> 00:15:18,510
Every single person Lee Woo Gyeom killed
was a criminal.

223
00:15:19,210 --> 00:15:20,210
What?

224
00:15:22,780 --> 00:15:24,950
If the victims were
these kinds of criminals,

225
00:15:25,010 --> 00:15:27,850
there's no way Prosecutor Cha didn't know.

226
00:15:30,020 --> 00:15:33,760
Since she hasn't made a move,
maybe she doesn't think it's a big deal.

227
00:15:34,560 --> 00:15:38,260
Legally, that he killed heinous criminals
doesn't help his case.

228
00:15:40,430 --> 00:15:41,530
That's true.

229
00:15:48,370 --> 00:15:50,070
- Reporter Jo.
- Yes?

230
00:15:51,410 --> 00:15:52,880
Isn't this a great story?

231
00:15:55,740 --> 00:15:58,610
- Why?
- Come on. You're being dense.

232
00:15:59,110 --> 00:16:00,780
Did you forget you're a reporter?

233
00:16:01,320 --> 00:16:04,920
Didn't you write he wasn't a murderer,
all for the clicks?

234
00:16:05,250 --> 00:16:07,060
Hey, that was a little...

235
00:16:10,760 --> 00:16:11,790
Oh, right!

236
00:16:12,760 --> 00:16:16,060
- So the victims are the real culprits?
- Exactly.

237
00:16:16,230 --> 00:16:18,870
The victims' criminal records...

238
00:16:20,100 --> 00:16:21,140
Leak them as well.

